(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-3-pulm-nosocomial-pneumonia-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Nosocomial Pneumonia & Its Antibiotics Quiz 2 — Pharmacology I Exam 3","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Why is ertapenem a limited-spectrum choice rather than a broad one in nosocomial pneumonia?","choices":["Rapid development of resistance","Poor penetration into lung tissue","Limited activity against Pseudomonas","A high risk of seizures"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Ertapenem is a narrow-spectrum carbapenem with limited activity against Pseudomonas aeruginosa, so it belongs to limited-spectrum coverage. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which cephalosporin is a fourth-generation antipseudomonal agent?","choices":["Ceftriaxone","Cefuroxime","Cefotaxime","Cefepime"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cefepime (Maxipime) is a fourth-generation antipseudomonal cephalosporin, and, like ceftazidime, it requires renal adjustment. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"In broad-spectrum coverage, an antipseudomonal beta-lactam is paired with which second gram-negative agent?","choices":["Moxifloxacin","Ciprofloxacin","Ceftriaxone","Linezolid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The antipseudomonal beta-lactam is combined with an antipseudomonal fluoroquinolone (levofloxacin or ciprofloxacin) or an aminoglycoside. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which regimen fits broad-spectrum empiric coverage for nosocomial pneumonia with risk factors for multidrug-resistant pathogens?","choices":["Meropenem and ciprofloxacin","Ceftriaxone and azithromycin","Cefepime, tobramycin and vancomycin","Ertapenem, levofloxacin and linezolid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Broad coverage is an antipseudomonal beta-lactam (cefepime) plus an antipseudomonal fluoroquinolone or aminoglycoside (tobramycin) plus vancomycin or linezolid. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What do cefepime and ceftazidime both require?","choices":["Seizure precautions","Trough monitoring","Renal adjustment","Hepatic adjustment"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Both antipseudomonal cephalosporins require renal adjustment, as do the antipseudomonal carbapenems, penicillins and fluoroquinolones. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A patient develops hospital-acquired pneumonia on hospital day 3 and has no risk factors for multidrug-resistant pathogens. Which initial approach fits?","choices":["Broad-spectrum therapy","Limited-spectrum therapy","Await the sputum culture","Supportive care alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. With early onset (under 5 days) and no multidrug-resistant risk factors, limited-spectrum initial antibiotic therapy is chosen. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Besides Pseudomonas, the antipseudomonal fluoroquinolones have good activity against which group of pathogens?","choices":["Atypical bacteria","Fungal pathogens","Resistant staphylococci","Anaerobic bacteria"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ciprofloxacin and levofloxacin have good activity against the atypicals (Mycoplasma, Chlamydia, Legionella) and good lung tissue penetration. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which history meets a listed criterion for health-care associated pneumonia?","choices":["Wound care six months ago","A hospital stay last year","Outpatient hemodialysis","A child in day care at home"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Health-care associated pneumonia criteria include hemodialysis, nursing home residence, recent intravenous antibiotics, chemotherapy, wound care in the previous 30 days and a recent hospital stay. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"When should antibiotic therapy for suspected nosocomial pneumonia begin?","choices":["After culture results return","After the sputum gram stain","After 48 hours of observation","As soon as possible"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Therapy starts as soon as possible, without waiting on a definitive culture, with broad-spectrum antibiotics that consider multidrug-resistant pathogens. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which finding is listed as a complication that causes failure of pneumonia therapy?","choices":["Neoplasm","Pulmonary embolus","Resistant pathogens","Lung abscess"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Complications causing failure are Clostridioides difficile colitis, empyema or lung abscess; a pulmonary embolus or neoplasm is a wrong diagnosis. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Why is a patient switched to oral antibiotics when possible during pneumonia treatment?","choices":["Better lung penetration","Broader coverage","Lower cost","Fewer resistant organisms"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Antibiotics are switched to oral when or if possible because oral therapy is cheaper, as coverage is scaled back to the culture results. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which history counts as a risk factor for multidrug-resistant pathogens in hospital-acquired pneumonia?","choices":["Immunosuppressive therapy","Hospitalization in winter","Depressed consciousness","Large-volume aspiration"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Risk factors for multidrug-resistant pathogens are prior antibiotics, colonization, hospitalization or chronic care, and immunosuppressive disease or therapy. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which hospitalized group has a higher incidence of nosocomial pneumonia?","choices":["Young adults after minor surgery","Patients with recent acute bronchitis","Patients discharged within one day","Mechanically ventilated patients"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Nosocomial pneumonia has a higher incidence in elderly and mechanically ventilated patients, with higher mortality and hospital costs. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which drug is an aminoglycoside?","choices":["Amikacin","Azithromycin","Ampicillin","Ertapenem"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The aminoglycosides are gentamicin, tobramycin and amikacin, used as a second gram-negative agent in broad coverage. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"How does nosocomial pneumonia rank among infections in hospitalized patients?","choices":["First, ahead of urinary tract infection","Second, after urinary tract infection","Second, after wound infection","Third, after urinary and wound infection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nosocomial pneumonia is the second most common infection in hospitalized patients, after urinary tract infection, with higher mortality and costs. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A patient on a ventilator develops pneumonia and has risk factors for multidrug-resistant pathogens. Which empiric regimen is appropriate?","choices":["Meropenem, gentamicin and linezolid","Imipenem-cilastatin, cefepime and vancomycin","Vancomycin, linezolid and ciprofloxacin","Ceftriaxone, tobramycin and vancomycin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Meropenem is an antipseudomonal carbapenem, gentamicin an aminoglycoside as the second gram-negative agent, and linezolid the gram-positive coverage. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"On what are aminoglycoside dose and frequency adjusted?","choices":["Measured drug levels","Liver function tests","Sputum gram stain","White blood cell count"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Aminoglycoside dose and frequency are adjusted based on levels, and they are highly dependent on renal function. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which antipseudomonal agent is used with caution in a patient with a seizure history?","choices":["Meropenem","Imipenem-cilastatin","Piperacillin-tazobactam","Tobramycin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Imipenem-cilastatin (Primaxin) is used with caution in patients with a seizure history; meropenem carries less seizure risk. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which drug used for nosocomial pneumonia needs no dose adjustment for renal function?","choices":["Vancomycin","Linezolid","Tobramycin","Cefepime"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Linezolid (Zyvox) needs no dose adjustment based on renal function; vancomycin, the aminoglycosides and the antipseudomonal beta-lactams all require renal adjustment. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"In limited-spectrum coverage for nosocomial pneumonia, which drug is chosen when atypical pathogens are a concern?","choices":["Ceftriaxone","Ampicillin-sulbactam","Ertapenem","Levofloxacin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Levofloxacin or moxifloxacin is the limited-spectrum choice when atypical pathogens are a worry; fluoroquinolones have good activity against Mycoplasma, Chlamydia and Legionella. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which two toxicities are listed for the aminoglycosides?","choices":["Hepatitis and hyperuricemia","Neuritis and lupus-like syndrome","Ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity","Seizures and arrhythmias"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Aminoglycosides cause ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity, and their dosing is highly dependent on renal function. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 28"}]};
